1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:05,238
This is my safe.

2
00:00:05,238 --> 00:00:06,167
There's no keypad.

3
00:00:06,167 --> 00:00:12,669
You press the button, show the camera three numbers with your fingers, and if they're right, the servo swings it open.

4
00:00:12,669 --> 00:00:16,694
The thing reading your fingers is a neural network I built from scratch.

5
00:00:16,694 --> 00:00:17,933
Here's how it works.

6
00:00:19,333 --> 00:00:23,195
The camera sees a picture, but a computer only sees numbers.

7
00:00:23,195 --> 00:00:28,461
I crop the middle of the frame and shrink it to 64 by 64 pixels.

8
00:00:28,461 --> 00:00:34,429
Each pixel has a red, a green and a blue value, so every picture is 12,288 numbers.

9
00:00:34,429 --> 00:00:41,100
The network's job is to turn those numbers into one answer: zero, one, two, three, four or five fingers.

10
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:44,436
The building block is a neuron.

11
00:00:44,436 --> 00:00:52,184
It multiplies each input by a weight, adds them all up, and passes the total through ReLU: negative becomes zero, positive goes straight through.

12
00:00:52,184 --> 00:00:57,349
That one bend is what lets a stack of these learn shapes, not just straight lines.

13
00:00:58,750 --> 00:01:00,689
For pictures I use a convolution.

14
00:01:00,689 --> 00:01:09,416
Instead of one neuron looking at the whole image, a tiny 3 by 3 filter, just nine weights, slides across the picture one step at a time.

15
00:01:09,416 --> 00:01:14,264
At every spot it multiplies and adds, and writes one number onto a new map.

16
00:01:14,264 --> 00:01:22,668
If the filter has learned to find an edge, the map lights up wherever there's an edge, anywhere in the picture, using the same nine weights.

17
00:01:22,668 --> 00:01:27,516
That sharing is why my first layer needs only 448 weights instead of almost 200,000.

18
00:01:28,916 --> 00:01:33,757
One filter finds one pattern, so each layer has many of them.

19
00:01:33,757 --> 00:01:34,967
That's the width.

20
00:01:34,967 --> 00:01:44,245
My first layer has 16 filters, so it makes 16 feature maps: edges at different angles, light against dark, skin against the background.

21
00:01:44,245 --> 00:01:48,683
Each layer after that doubles it: 32, then 64, then 128.

22
00:01:50,083 --> 00:01:52,043
After each convolution comes max pooling.

23
00:01:52,043 --> 00:01:56,618
I look at every 2 by 2 block and keep only the biggest number.

24
00:01:56,618 --> 00:02:03,481
The map shrinks to half its width and height, 64, then 32, 16 and 8, so there's far less to compute.

25
00:02:03,481 --> 00:02:10,016
And because it keeps the strongest signal, a finger that moves over by one pixel gives almost the same answer.

26
00:02:11,416 --> 00:02:13,058
Stacking layers is the depth.

27
00:02:13,058 --> 00:02:25,868
My network has four convolution layers, and each one looks at the one below it, so it sees a bigger patch of the original picture: 3 pixels, then 8, then 18, then 38, enough to see a whole hand.

28
00:02:25,868 --> 00:02:30,795
Edges become bits of finger, bits become fingers, and fingers become "three in a row".

29
00:02:30,795 --> 00:02:38,350
In my tests, depth mattered most: with only two layers, the network couldn't see a whole hand and got less than half right.

30
00:02:39,750 --> 00:02:51,037
At the end, global average pooling squashes each of the 128 maps down to one number, and a last layer turns those into six scores, one for each count.

31
00:02:51,037 --> 00:02:56,097
Softmax turns the scores into probabilities, and the biggest one is the answer.

32
00:02:56,097 --> 00:02:58,433
Altogether, the network has 98,694 weights.

33
00:02:59,833 --> 00:03:03,141
At first the weights are random, so the answers are garbage.

34
00:03:03,141 --> 00:03:04,043
Training fixes that.

35
00:03:04,043 --> 00:03:09,156
I show it a picture and the right answer, and the loss measures how wrong it was.

36
00:03:09,156 --> 00:03:19,683
Backpropagation then works backwards through every layer using the chain rule, and figures out, for every single weight: if I nudge you a little, does the loss go up or down, and by how much?

37
00:03:21,083 --> 00:03:25,341
Gradient descent uses that answer to move every weight a small step downhill.

38
00:03:25,341 --> 00:03:32,876
Do that thousands of times, on batches of 64 pictures, and the loss rolls down into a valley where the answers are right.

39
00:03:32,876 --> 00:03:39,100
I wrote that step myself, with momentum, so it keeps rolling through the small bumps instead of getting stuck.

40
00:03:40,500 --> 00:03:45,098
It learned from about 2,500 pictures of my hand, taken with the webcam.

41
00:03:45,098 --> 00:03:51,111
During training every picture gets randomly turned, zoomed, mirrored and re-lit, so it can't just memorise them.

42
00:03:51,111 --> 00:03:59,600
Then I test it on recordings it has never seen: it gets 95 percent right, and every count from one to five was perfect.

43
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,620
Here's something we didn't do in class: Grad-CAM.

44
00:04:03,620 --> 00:04:08,535
It uses backpropagation again, but asks a different question: which pixels made you say that?

45
00:04:08,535 --> 00:04:10,500
The answer is a heat map.

46
00:04:10,500 --> 00:04:12,466
And it caught my network cheating.

47
00:04:12,466 --> 00:04:17,380
It had decided my smartwatch meant "one", because I wore it in my "one" pictures.

48
00:04:17,380 --> 00:04:23,933
I recorded more pictures with and without the watch, and now the heat sits on the fingers, where it belongs.

49
00:04:25,333 --> 00:04:29,302
Last, I moved the trained network onto the Arduino UNO Q.

50
00:04:29,302 --> 00:04:35,798
It reads the camera about fifteen times a second, and the network takes about 25 milliseconds per picture.

51
00:04:35,798 --> 00:04:39,045
So: press the button, the lights come on, one…

52
00:04:39,045 --> 00:04:39,406
four…

53
00:04:39,406 --> 00:04:39,767
two…

54
00:04:39,767 --> 00:04:40,850
and it's open.

